[al:新概念英语（四）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:Galileo Reborn]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.96]Lesson 32 | 第32课
[00:02.85]Galileo reborn | 伽利略的复生
[00:10.06]What has modified our traditional view of Galileo in recent times? | 近来是什么改变了我们对伽利略的传统看法？
[00:17.77]In his own lifetime Galileo was the centre of violent controversy, but the scientific dust has long since settled, | 伽利略在世时是激烈论战的中心人物，但关于他的科学尘埃早已落定，
[00:26.84]and today we can see even his famous clash with the Inquisition in something like its proper perspective. | 今天我们甚至能以比较正确的眼光来看待他同宗教裁判所的著名冲突。
[00:34.42]But, in contrast, it is only in modern times that Galileo has become a problem child for historians of science. | 但是，与此形成对照的是，只是在现代，伽利略才成为科学史家眼中的一个问题儿童。
[00:44.11]The old view of Galileo was delightfully uncomplicated. | 过去对伽利略的看法是令人愉快地简单。
[00:48.63]He was, above all, a man who experimented: | 他首先是一个做实验的人，
[00:52.78]who despised the prejudice and book learning of the Aristotelians, | 一个蔑视亚里士多德学派的偏见和书本知识的人，
[00:57.49]who put his questions to nature instead of to the ancients, and who drew his conclusions fearlessly. | 一个向自然界而不是向古人提出问题、并且无所畏惧地下结论的人。
[01:05.65]He had been the first to turn a telescope to the sky, | 他是第一个把望远镜指向天空的人，
[01:09.37]and he had seen there evidence enough to overthrow Aristotle and Ptolemy together. | 他在那里看到了足以同时推翻亚里士多德和托勒密的证据。
[01:15.98]He was the man who climbed the Leaning Tower of Pisa and dropped various weights from the top, | 他就是那个爬上比萨斜塔、从塔顶抛下各种重物的人，
[01:22.40]who rolled balls down inclined planes, and then generalized the results of his many experiments into the famous law of free fall. | 就是那个让小球沿斜面滚下、然后把多次实验的结果归纳为著名的自由落体定律的人。
[01:33.86]But a closer study of the evidence, | 但是，对证据的更仔细的研究，
[01:36.04]supported by a deeper sense of the period, and particularly by a new consciousness of the philosophical undercurrents in the scientific revolution, | 加上对那个时代的更深入的理解，特别是对科学革命中哲学潜流的新的认识，
[01:45.78]has profoundly modified this view of Galileo. | 已经深刻地改变了这种对伽利略的看法。
[01:49.86]Today, although the old Galileo lives on in many popular writings, among historians of science a new and more sophisticated picture has emerged. | 今天，虽然老一套的伽利略形象仍活在许多通俗读物中，但在科学史家中间，一幅新的、更复杂的图景已经出现。
[02:01.49]At the same time our sympathy for Galileo's opponents has grown somewhat. | 与此同时，我们对伽利略的反对者们的同情也有所增长。
[02:06.42]His telescopic observations are justly immortal; | 他的望远镜观测成果是理所当然地不朽的，
[02:10.41]they aroused great interest at the time, | 它们当时激起了极大的兴趣，
[02:13.17]they had important theoretical consequences, | 具有重要的理论意义，
[02:16.37]and they provided a striking demonstration of the potentialities hidden in instruments and apparatus. | 并且引人注目地展示了隐藏在仪器和装置中的潜力。
[02:24.60]But can we blame those who looked and failed to see what Galileo saw, | 但是，那些看了却没有看出伽利略所看到的东西的人能受责怪吗，
[02:30.04]if we remember that to use a telescope at the limit of its powers calls for long experience and intimate familiarity with one's instrument? | 如果我们记得，在望远镜极限功率下使用它需要长期的经验和对自己仪器的透彻熟悉？
[02:40.50]Was the philosopher who refused to look through Galileo's telescope more culpable than those who alleged | 那位拒绝透过伽利略的望远镜观看的哲学家，是否比那些声称
[02:47.50]that the spiral nebulae observed with Lord Rosse's great telescope in the 1840s were scratches left by the grinder? | 19世纪40年代用罗斯勋爵的巨大望远镜观察到的螺旋星云只是磨镜工留下的划痕的人更有罪责呢？
[02:57.31]We can perhaps forgive those who said the moons of Jupiter were produced by Galileo's spyglass if we recall that in his day, | 如果我们回想起，在他那个时代，
[03:06.67]as for centuries before, curved glass was the popular contrivance for producing not truth but illusion, untruth; | 正如此前几个世纪一样，曲面玻璃是制造幻觉而非真实的流行手法，那么我们也许可以原谅那些说木星的卫星是伽利略的小望远镜变出来的人；
[03:16.81]and if a single curved glass would distort nature, how much more would a pair of them? | 而如果一片曲面玻璃就会歪曲自然，那么两片又将会怎样呢？
